#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "18371fa9-696a-5a3a-b7e6-400f14d2fe62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6724" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n- $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n\n- $y''+ 4 = 0$\n\n- $y''+y'+4y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a constant term, $4.$\n\n- Equation III takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2\n$$\n\nand $b = 1.$ Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/id #uuid "365ec1d4-0301-5dfe-a731-35f12ea4d3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6725" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\n- $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\n- $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={1}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-7<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "0458947c-70c8-5f88-9e52-68c8950afd7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6726" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y .\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+15y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "c93a18f3-6d09-5d88-8a90-434fc08d3874" :question/math-academy-id "e-6727" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 2y' + 5y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3d7e5c7-643a-588f-a004-8c36a48674c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146696} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ecade0-3279-5045-82f7-0f9c4dddb348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} + 4y = 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146697} {:answer/id #uuid "4971ef4c-37c7-5c03-a0a7-382319085e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} - y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146698} {:answer/id #uuid "52bdc76c-7f80-51d7-9e76-f2008052eabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = \\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146699} {:answer/id #uuid "67cbbeb5-7390-5f4f-a57b-ceeb2996c173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67cbbeb5-7390-5f4f-a57b-ceeb2996c173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146700} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce631ee-73ed-54e6-b738-45ea8c5e7810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb285096-2d90-5156-8e1d-ee98188e9337" :question/math-academy-id "q-64538" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8288310b-29ff-527d-baea-630972cff6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} + 10y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146708} {:answer/id #uuid "ac226551-e9ad-5b36-8912-12fdb1ece833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146709} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2b7bf5-e991-5ca4-89df-00bd19c56100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} + \\sqrt{5}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146710} {:answer/id #uuid "5c176f36-5eaa-5feb-a824-3dfb155c192e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} + 5y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146711} {:answer/id #uuid "6590e2c3-8eef-5488-8a43-7ac9ea63e24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + \\sqrt{5}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac226551-e9ad-5b36-8912-12fdb1ece833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146709} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9384eb-84c6-56e6-baee-d56943289c9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39f1ef46-09c9-5bbf-b30f-92ddff3f269a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64544" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=4\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-4{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc9c357-78b4-5e94-94f4-f5386a0fcd19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}πs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146714} {:answer/id #uuid "a9862c3a-723f-5e65-8035-513384b7028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146715} {:answer/id #uuid "28e2632d-9de1-512e-ab97-5b5893c75c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{\\sqrt{3}}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146716} {:answer/id #uuid "57b3e71b-cc10-5f64-b173-37d1fa223677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146717} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4aafeb-b4de-588c-a1ca-ec72da0d0cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "πs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b3e71b-cc10-5f64-b173-37d1fa223677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146717} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0755965-8ff8-52a7-9546-a0263d2fe969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15584f20-fb01-5c6f-87f6-787ded6f6315" :question/math-academy-id "q-64547" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=3\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f5d6d2c-67a7-5337-affd-a701ca5ff9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146768} {:answer/id #uuid "bb892b85-4255-5b98-a079-4791589c3e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146769} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bfcf06-f860-5cf0-b6b6-ef5d2af5da10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146770} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3e3950-b1d7-5763-aff8-3c8febde5bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146771} {:answer/id #uuid "d71588f2-de9b-5253-9d49-5e5a5cf4b262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3e3950-b1d7-5763-aff8-3c8febde5bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146771} :answer-field/id #uuid "697a5cfb-ce1d-55a3-a785-5d3ce7804013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55cfa82a-9072-54fc-bfe1-353f6a508e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-64576" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={10}^{2}-4(1)(25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =13>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdf4889-a09a-512d-b9dc-835c29c13b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+18y=5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205} {:answer/id #uuid "c46993fb-268e-5935-9d2c-4ac8efdec827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348206} {:answer/id #uuid "d67337a8-e843-5b4c-b20f-2b947f6afe52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-16y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348207} {:answer/id #uuid "9799c77c-9bfd-5e94-b3b8-367f268cbf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}-9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:answer/id #uuid "72fdac93-0bfa-5e20-8163-430c8021b28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-25y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c46993fb-268e-5935-9d2c-4ac8efdec827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348206} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f61d78-9c5c-5056-9862-d7703a0dfb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18c0275c-179c-553e-bebe-a7b02a771fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64588" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e74de20d-fc2b-5253-916a-6d785666ce13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{5}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348212} {:answer/id #uuid "77a7dd94-3cdc-5053-8cd2-f9c08c9018d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348213} {:answer/id #uuid "47fc8fef-a22c-50c6-8d29-250c6f6c8a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+15y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348214} {:answer/id #uuid "e0aad37b-12e4-535b-bba9-2259dc9f0787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{5}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348215} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0c77d6-2769-5d00-ad4b-2ef02ee6a8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad0c77d6-2769-5d00-ad4b-2ef02ee6a8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348216} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2b8e7e-797d-52c5-8baa-42263e848843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d91f4c4-3ef2-565b-a8e8-e2fe48379756" :question/math-academy-id "q-64591" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-15y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bed2b41-81b2-5498-93f2-350f6c7c2543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348219} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba0da2f-a66c-5d02-962e-59b3ba1b4d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348220} {:answer/id #uuid "afccdb7d-8f54-5811-a4c7-5b2cbcb7c77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348221} {:answer/id #uuid "62385843-2d0f-5289-94b0-d23d6ad18b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222} {:answer/id #uuid "270c727a-30b9-5a77-b4d9-dbcb663c407c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62385843-2d0f-5289-94b0-d23d6ad18b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fe6d592-d490-5f86-96c7-76785bc3fd19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "725a719a-f22a-5836-bd4b-c3c09e81ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-64592" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-8y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ee4659-76aa-5d40-8c17-7f66ff978701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348226} {:answer/id #uuid "bb879104-0d01-5ae3-80a7-05c4b7c4c05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348227} {:answer/id #uuid "123cc7f3-e1b1-5da0-b9b4-1bac44aed79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348228} {:answer/id #uuid "0b69f31a-8675-5368-a9c2-d6978f241b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348229} {:answer/id #uuid "4cef4e93-623d-5a37-b1bc-b9606e162994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123cc7f3-e1b1-5da0-b9b4-1bac44aed79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348228} :answer-field/id #uuid "843f15d2-dae7-5145-8dd3-1ca1f3a80ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3bc45b0-6823-5a51-8bde-47d5291bd4b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64615" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38255b87-3b9c-5d04-93c4-2306ca713e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348233} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdb8920-8478-5970-8954-cc107f5a65fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348234} {:answer/id #uuid "d600b204-4097-5140-bff8-9cc5f7e5de00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348235} {:answer/id #uuid "c206a65b-e4d2-5643-b1ca-fc22f849f7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348236} {:answer/id #uuid "9427e006-6b12-5911-95d0-c4f1103301e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d600b204-4097-5140-bff8-9cc5f7e5de00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348235} :answer-field/id #uuid "905f579c-e17c-5d3c-8636-bb6e705ab18e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3c2edca-263a-53db-b023-0f659e013834" :question/math-academy-id "q-64616" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(6) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =8>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "631e6161-44fd-5a1f-b025-2a9662e9e00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348240} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8299ad-de5c-5b17-8c80-f80edbe0e7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241} {:answer/id #uuid "856b0f8e-a840-56ac-83ab-0f2e7917c7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348242} {:answer/id #uuid "2720dcd6-74df-5c84-b5c9-ef700009021d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348243} {:answer/id #uuid "719c9ef1-c7cd-56b6-9a5c-b68f9652d1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631e6161-44fd-5a1f-b025-2a9662e9e00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c78593-2e93-5825-9e70-0fb3a5a4fa3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e801805-48d7-5f7f-a2eb-42e2eeee70b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64623" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=6\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c208fd24-8ddd-539d-a540-a8a9662d1c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348247} {:answer/id #uuid "462f5ab7-16e1-5bf1-bb16-304d9b66a8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348248} {:answer/id #uuid "c14cc2d3-2a61-52b0-99ef-9375858a6112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348249} {:answer/id #uuid "3befb438-27b3-5983-95d6-de399dede742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348250} {:answer/id #uuid "a978f518-5c77-52f8-8550-22772131b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a978f518-5c77-52f8-8550-22772131b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348251} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc725aa-213a-5738-9c22-e72e2b431475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d46e5e1-bf5a-599b-9a5f-cddcbbd94b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-79498" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeb439f7-6139-587d-8c92-791296d0512f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348254} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a9bb00-4f2e-58db-8068-af2adac965a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348255} {:answer/id #uuid "54e25263-a4f2-5ae3-8592-1e8a031333b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348256} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc1b9a1-4eb3-542b-9375-d7e5c5bc6c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348257} {:answer/id #uuid "b4773025-9a76-5a31-a08e-ff94e02a6eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4773025-9a76-5a31-a08e-ff94e02a6eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348258} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c14214d-b8ed-55a8-a768-0b75dd1b4df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f364ce71-ef4b-5e70-9cba-e414d0c6bb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-79570" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=45\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed and has a damping coefficient $c=20\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters) and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}=-20{y}^{′}-45y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca48112-3cc3-5800-b97c-61fc7775d06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261} {:answer/id #uuid "11766f48-f805-5881-9f09-0dca02fdab99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348262} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e59327-f8c2-5cde-af01-dcae20cffcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348263} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c414e8-f1b5-5e98-8e5f-3db726dc2eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348264} {:answer/id #uuid "caea2a49-747f-59af-95b6-36be02ad6f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e59327-f8c2-5cde-af01-dcae20cffcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348263} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb258585-a0e8-5484-9fc2-322b7d28f2d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5708124f-9743-5ab7-8b57-9c32436ebfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79571" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=5\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27471f2b-3615-5cfc-9e37-8d03453240b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348268} {:answer/id #uuid "7c001a30-4251-5c42-9849-fa67d6e2cd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348269} {:answer/id #uuid "a04927e5-7e3d-566c-a619-9159691f1f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348270} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4ffbdc-2b8a-52d9-9c54-08526a949af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348271} {:answer/id #uuid "379770c3-8199-57da-b65a-df3a22c49fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4ffbdc-2b8a-52d9-9c54-08526a949af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348271} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6d393a-4ae6-5e4b-91c1-9b3bfd2977e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d2256ea-4577-552a-9156-47e04ecd47ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-94295" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2b9c062-9427-5631-9e46-acf289a4d345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348275} {:answer/id #uuid "44eeaf3a-b90e-50d8-a1ad-87870dc9ae42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-25y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276} {:answer/id #uuid "45199ba8-21b0-50f9-817c-1a5fbf26d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348277} {:answer/id #uuid "422f59af-b798-5b74-9175-2a90e78f1e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348278} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a6dadb-d4d5-586f-aba8-bb394524a3cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=2{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "422f59af-b798-5b74-9175-2a90e78f1e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348278} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fcc32e-ded6-5718-9d4a-ac06c323d89d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d13c1d0e-304a-57f1-9e31-5404f6ab6e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-94298" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718f5686-4ad9-52d5-ac0a-54316f566477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348282} {:answer/id #uuid "df86a44a-a8d0-503f-8be3-ddb02af6fed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348283} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a98e5-54ca-5f15-80aa-5244945db197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348284} {:answer/id #uuid "0ecab809-b4be-5f10-83ea-dcdbac318073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348285} {:answer/id #uuid "c7059fb7-b7dc-5c66-9768-5f1f49f22a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a98e5-54ca-5f15-80aa-5244945db197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348284} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df7941f-142c-59d5-bdd9-c46b468d1bad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c740c4a-060a-582d-bc23-6130ccbffc30" :question/math-academy-id "q-94310" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c60ebaf9-f5ab-55fd-825e-5f3e33a39032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348289} {:answer/id #uuid "86223b95-4c68-58cf-8178-e934373c75f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348290} {:answer/id #uuid "55bb50ad-6a9f-5c31-8a8e-c3a1890e3c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348291} {:answer/id #uuid "80c708b4-3f9f-5b75-a95f-28f50f7e1db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348292} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8dd74d-15bf-52eb-99f6-cb5a52f2d98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55bb50ad-6a9f-5c31-8a8e-c3a1890e3c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348291} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba46316d-1657-55e6-8433-b9772de5c104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66e2419b-81b9-577c-8d4a-f28601be4294" :question/math-academy-id "q-94313" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4(1)(2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =13>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +14=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(14) & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2740634a-c4f3-57b1-9914-d4a698804684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348296} {:answer/id #uuid "b01e9ae7-ec14-5609-89b0-8d8d140ac7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348297} {:answer/id #uuid "8555aad5-fd47-5fe6-a25f-cb342ad2bf78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348298} {:answer/id #uuid "c96ce911-b8db-5786-85f0-05ff782da121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{10}\\,y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348299} {:answer/id #uuid "b4977280-7fb1-51d0-a4e7-bfde9e393136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4977280-7fb1-51d0-a4e7-bfde9e393136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348300} :answer-field/id #uuid "4caaa4a2-fa50-5ffe-a2a7-cd50a5a1602f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d271849-ab2b-5594-87c7-ec48335ece54" :question/math-academy-id "q-94321" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=8\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-8{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000dd01b-66c8-5323-b95f-d45f11ce7ad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348303} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3a3ff6-7463-5b4e-bb9f-705dcd1617bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348304} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1431e6-71b0-578d-91d6-3f7db55531c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348305} {:answer/id #uuid "0abd775f-1ef8-58d9-b1e4-0ee03068d89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348306} {:answer/id #uuid "d91aadeb-76e5-540e-825c-957af8d5efaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3a3ff6-7463-5b4e-bb9f-705dcd1617bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348304} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d222cbf-9f04-584b-ba89-863147a0b875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef47c539-b602-58eb-b569-f1401213dfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94362" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=2\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12724f4f-4528-5ced-8b7f-2bacdf4ac570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348310} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7fd7f8-1b5e-5444-9bbb-05440fee470d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348311} {:answer/id #uuid "78369663-0d16-5232-9cb2-0d97f1dfab02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348312} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf28bc8-dddb-5818-a4f5-1c04a329fdf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348313} {:answer/id #uuid "b9dcf63e-c329-5f14-a58a-306dc86710c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9dcf63e-c329-5f14-a58a-306dc86710c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348314} :answer-field/id #uuid "a695bae9-4941-5639-8b31-eab8d5a00568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "761f3793-8037-5809-bc55-68013ff1f2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94363" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36)-36+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +6 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-6t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=4\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}]}
